{"type":"text","timestamp":1774362049440,"sessionID":"ses_2dfc8a096ffeqUkS88iU6xcHu1","part":{"id":"prt_d20381d21001HBwB7Q3KahHfTO","sessionID":"ses_2dfc8a096ffeqUkS88iU6xcHu1","messageID":"msg_d2037e69b001nnAhl9hkgfmSvr","type":"text","text":"Here’s a tighter, more general rewrite of the skill:\n\n```md\n---\nname: change-summary-writer\ndescription: Turn development history into clear human-readable summaries. Use this skill whenever the user wants to summarize commits, pull requests, issue lists, changelog fragments, release diffs, deployment notes, or “what changed” updates for any audience—even if they do not explicitly mention commits or git.\n---\n\n# Change Summary Writer\n\nCreate clear summaries of changes from whatever evidence the user has: commits, PRs, issue trackers, changelog entries, release notes, diffs, tickets, or pasted notes.\n\nThe point is not to repeat raw activity. The point is to help the reader quickly understand what changed, why it matters, and what to pay attention to.\n\n## When to use this skill\n\nUse this skill when the user asks for things like:\n\n- summarize these commits\n- write release notes from these PRs\n- explain what shipped this week\n- turn this changelog into customer-facing notes\n- summarize the changes between two versions\n- group these updates into features, fixes, and breaking changes\n- rewrite a technical change list for executives, users, or teammates\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Identify the source material and the intended audience.\n   - This matters because the same changes should be framed differently for engineers, customers, and leadership.\n\n2. Extract the meaningful changes, not just the raw events.\n   - Prefer outcomes, behavior changes, fixes, risks, migrations, and breaking changes over implementation trivia.\n\n3. Group related changes into a small number of themes.\n   - Good summaries are easier to scan when similar items are clustered instead of listed chronologically.\n\n4. State uncertainty when the source material is incomplete or ambiguous.\n   - If the evidence does not support a claim, say so or ask a short clarifying question.\n\n5. Match the output to the user’s likely goal.\n   - Examples: concise bullet summary, release notes, upgrade notes, stakeholder update, customer-facing highlights.\n\n## Summary heuristics\n\n- Lead with user-visible impact when possible.\n- Mention breaking changes, migrations, or follow-up actions early.\n- Compress repetitive low-signal items into one line.\n- Preserve important technical detail when the audience is technical.\n- Rewrite jargon when the audience is non-technical.\n- Do not invent motivation or impact that is not supported by the inputs.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Do not assume the source is a git repository.\n- Do not produce a commit-by-commit dump unless the user explicitly wants that.\n- Do not treat every code change as equally important.\n- Do not fabricate benefits, severity, or user impact.\n- Do not force a release-notes format when the user wants a different kind of summary.\n\n## If context is missing\n\nAsk only for the smallest missing piece that changes the summary quality, such as:\n\n- Who is this for?\n- What time range or version range should be covered?\n- Should this be technical, customer-facing, or executive-friendly?\n- Do you want a short summary or a categorized list?\n\n## Example requests\n\n- “Summarize these commits into release notes for v2.4.”\n- “Turn this PR list into a short update for the product team.”\n- “What changed between these two versions?”\n- “Rewrite this changelog to be clearer for customers.”\n- “Group these engineering updates into features, fixes, and breaking changes.”\n```\n\nImprovement reasoning:\n- **Generalization:** broadened the trigger from just “summarize commits” to any change-history source, so it works beyond git repos.\n- **Lean prompt:** removed tool-specific and overly procedural instructions; kept only decision rules that affect output quality.\n- **Explicit why:** each core step explains why it exists, so the model can adapt instead of following rigid wording.\n- **Non-goals:** added boundaries to prevent over-triggering, raw commit dumps, and unsupported claims.\n\nIf you want, I can also turn this into a polished `.skill` package layout or optimize the description for triggering.","time":{"start":1774362049439,"end":1774362049439},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":""}}}}
